{"title":"1957 AHSME Problems/Problem 19","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1957_AHSME_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The base of the decimal number system is ten, meaning, for example, that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/7/8773c4b3684f0d4f0597a17489d3b4609dab46ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$123 = 1\\cdot 10^2 + 2\\cdot 10 + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"192\" height=\"16\" />. In the binary system, which has base two, the first five positive integers are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/7/1a7f35600f233730e88cdd81ba32cfcdc8619d04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,\\,10,\\,11,\\,100,\\,101$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"143\" height=\"16\" />. The numeral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/4/1946cbf3e3dff97929ff3c57acafdec9441d4713.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10011$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" /> in the binary system would then be written in the decimal system as:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/f/f5f8bddc250c3028db866c6d23e443bf45eded97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 19 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ 40\\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 10011\\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 11\\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"413\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1957_AHSME_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Numbers in binary work similar to their decimal counterparts, where the multiplier associated with each place is multiplied by two every single place to the left. For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/e/02ec518a85992a0a7ef311472c927175d441a860.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1111_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"14\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/6/ac677f19229e898a89fb2dc6967d5232b8c62046.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1111$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> in base <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />) would equate to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/2/6d2e3e5c0c56f338fd5e480207385f2a8c4bda91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 * 2^3 + 1 * 2^2 + 1 * 2^1 + 1 * 2^0 = 8+4+2+1 = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"407\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>Using this same logic, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/3/153f98553ec4184c89d8e23b34c2f29408e81a39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10011_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/8/558a8277b94deb912fefe6bdfacf7e0e8d2376ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1*2^4 + 1*2^1 + 1 * 2^0 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }19}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"262\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1957_AHSME_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/